---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'How do I know if my PDF form is fillable?', a: 'Open it in PDFSherpa’s Fill PDF Form tool. If it lists fields, it’s fillable. If it says there are no fields, it’s a flat (scanned or printed-to-PDF) form, so use Edit PDF to type on it.' },
  { q: 'Can I save a half-filled form and continue later?', a: 'Yes. Leave “Lock answers” unticked, download the file, and open it again later to change or add answers.' },
  { q: 'Do I need Adobe Acrobat?', a: 'No. For standard fillable forms a browser is enough. Only old XFA forms need Adobe Acrobat Reader.' },
];
---
<GuideLayout slug="fill-pdf-form-online" faqs={faqs}>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> open <a href="/fill-pdf-form">Fill PDF Form</a>, select your form, type your answers into the fields that appear, tick <em>Lock answers</em> if you’re submitting it, and save. If the PDF has no fillable fields, use <a href="/edit-pdf">Edit PDF</a> to type anywhere on the page. Then sign it with <a href="/sign-pdf">Sign PDF</a>.</p>

  <h2>Two kinds of PDF forms</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fillable forms</strong> have real fields built in, such as text boxes, checkboxes and drop-downs. They’re made in tools like Word, Acrobat or online form builders.</li>
    <li><strong>Flat forms</strong> are just pictures of a form, usually scans or “printed to PDF”. There are no fields, so you type on top of the page instead.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Filling a fillable form</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <a href="/fill-pdf-form">Fill PDF Form</a> and select the PDF.</li>
    <li>Fields appear as a simple form, grouped by page. On a phone, this is much easier than zooming into tiny boxes.</li>
    <li>Fill in text, tick checkboxes, choose from drop-downs.</li>
    <li>Tick <strong>Lock answers (flatten)</strong> if you’re sending it to an organisation, so nothing gets changed by accident.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Save filled PDF</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="guide-article" />

  <h2>Filling a flat form</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <a href="/edit-pdf">Edit PDF</a> and select the form.</li>
    <li>Tap <strong>Add text</strong>, type your answer, and drag it onto the line or box. Resize it with the blue dot.</li>
    <li>Use the same font size for every answer so the form looks neat. 10–12 pt usually fits printed forms.</li>
    <li>Save and download.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Signing and sending</h2>
  <p>
    Open the filled form in <a href="/sign-pdf">Sign PDF</a>, add your signature and the date, and download. If the portal has a size limit, run it through
    <a href="/compress-pdf">Compress PDF</a>. If you need to attach documents, combine them with <a href="/merge-pdf">Merge PDF</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep your details private</h2>
  <p>
    Forms contain your ID number, address and date of birth. PDFSherpa fills, edits and signs them inside your browser. Nothing is uploaded, so your
    information goes only where you choose to send it.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
